-- title:
--   Integral power series from a retraction and a cotangent generator
-- statement:
--   Let $R$, $R'$ and $L$ be commutative rings with $L$ an $R$-algebra, and let $\iota \colon R \to R'$ and $\pi \colon R' \to R$ be ring homomorphisms with $\pi \circ \iota = \mathrm{id}_R$, so that $\pi$ retracts $\iota$. Write $I = \ker \pi$. Assume given $t \in R'$ with $\pi t = 0$ (that is, $t \in I$) such that $I \le (t) + I^2$, where $(t)$ is the ideal generated by $t$; thus the class of $t$ generates $I/I^2$. Let $e \colon R' \to L[[X]]$ be a ring homomorphism such that $e(\iota r)$ is the constant series with value $\mathrm{algebraMap}_{R,L}(r)$ for every $r \in R$, and such that the constant coefficient of $e(i)$ vanishes for every $i \in I$. Assume finally that $e(t)$ is the coefficientwise image in $L[[X]]$ of some $u \in R[[X]]$. Then for every $z \in R'$ there is a power series $P \in R[[X]]$ whose coefficientwise image in $L[[X]]$ equals $e(z)$ and whose constant coefficient is $\pi z$. The hypothesis that $e(t)$ comes from $u$ is used through the cited digit lemma; $u$ itself is otherwise unconstrained.
--
--   This is the commutative-algebra core of the $q$-expansion principle for functions: $R'$ plays the role of the local ring of a smooth curve over $R$ along a section, $\pi$ of evaluation along that section, $t$ of a parameter cutting the section out to first order, and $e$ of the $q$-expansion map. It is used in the treatment of the cusp $\infty$ on the modular curve, via [`ModularCurve.exists_powerSeries_map_eq_ffEquiv_symm_of_placeOfPoint_eq_cuspInftyFull`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_powerSeries_map_eq_ffEquiv_symm_of_placeOfPoint_eq_cuspInftyFull).
